SC["a5c31_door"]={img:"door",
 t:()=>SYS(`CHAPTER THIRTY-ONE — THE DOOR BETWEEN, STANDING OPEN`)+P(`When the sky has changed hands and the city has begun, awkwardly, joyfully, to learn its new arithmetic, you walk the master warp one last time to the selvage, and the Door Between is not a metaphor tonight, and not locked, and not even shut. Your own gold line runs through it, off the hem of this heaven, across the dark, to a weave the color of sodium lights and rain.`)+((G.f.a5_loom=="woven")?(P(`The new pattern sings behind you — the chord, the rota, the ring, a mended sky that needs its weaver the way a healthy village needs its doctor: regularly, and not desperately. You could go. For the first time since a riverbank, going wouldn't be desertion.`)):(""))+((G.f.a5_loom=="free")?(P(`Behind you, an empty valley and a seam of promises — a sky held by watch-shifts and freely-given knots, no loom, no center, no single thread anything hangs on. Including yours. You could go. The world you're standing in finally holds without anyone being indispensable, which was the entire point.`)):(""))+((G.f.a5_loom=="heir")?(P(`The heart-seat holds you the way you hold it: living, woven, the pattern's own grammar. You cannot leave, and that was the bargain, taken with open eyes. But a Sage woven living can do what four hundred years of dead pattern never could: SPLIT the line. The boy and the heart. The stitch that goes and the stitch that stays. The Lady taught you, in her last reel, exactly how she never managed it.`)):(""))+P(`Souls drift in the dark beyond it: the next foreigners, falling slowly toward thin places. The porch light burns on the far hem. The choice is the last one, and it is entirely, finally yours.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`STEP THROUGH. Home — the rain, the street, the light left on. Carry everything; owe nothing; let two worlds keep their own skies.`,do:()=>{G.f.a5_door="home";},go:"a5c31_go"},
  {id:"c1",l:`HOLD IT OPEN. Make the crossing a DOOR at last: sanctioned, tended, met: every soul the Between sends arrives into welcome instead of a net.`,do:()=>{G.f.a5_door="open";},go:"a5c31_go"},
  {id:"c2",l:`CLOSE IT FOREVER. No more arrivals, no more harvests, no more boys on riverbanks: this heaven keeps its own sky now, and the seam is sewn shut behind you.`,do:()=>{G.f.a5_door="shut";},go:"a5c31_go"}]};

SC.a5c31_go={t:()=>P("The door waits while you say what has to be said, and the saying takes until almost dawn."),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:"Dawn.",go:()=>"e_"+(G.f.a5_loom||"woven")+"_"+(G.f.a5_door||"home")}]};


SC["e_woven_home"]={img:"home",
 t:()=>P(`The goodbyes take three days, because you do them properly, and the door takes three steps, because it was always going to be easy once it was allowed to be.`)+P(`You walk your own gold line off the hem of a mended heaven — past the drifting souls, through the Between where you once hung loose and humming, and the last thing you hear from the world behind is the chord: ten thousand willing lines, holding without you, BECAUSE of you, which is the only kind of monument you ever wanted. Then rain. Sodium light. The bus shelter's plastic roof, hammering, exactly as loud as memory. The transformer across the street is silent, fixed years ago, no song in the wire at all.`)+P(`The porch light is on. Of course it is. You stand in the rain a moment — fourteen and four hundred, soaked and woven, a boy with an entire sky folded into him somewhere no one here will ever see. Then you walk up the path, and the door opens before you knock, because somebody who never once got evidence never once stopped checking the street.`)+P(`Behind you, forever, on the far side of a door between worlds: a sky that holds, a rota that turns, a ring of knots tied in every grammar but harvest. They'll tell it wrong on the river: the weaver who mended heaven and vanished. Let them. The stitches know. The Loom remembers.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Morning.`,go:"a5c32_coda"}]};

SC["e_woven_open"]={img:"heaven",
 t:()=>P(`You do not step through. You PROP it.`)+P(`It takes a season, and it is the new pattern's first great work: the Door Between, woven OPEN. Selvage folded back, your gold line braided into a permanent crossing-frame, and around it, on the world's side, the thing four centuries of theology said was impossible: a HARBOR. Mei drafts the receiving protocols like physic: warmth, language, time. The Reed Wife — who knows better than anyone alive what an unmet arrival costs — keeps the first watch, and the watch becomes an order, and the order takes her quiet as its whole rule.`)+P(`The next soul the Between sends arrives eleven days later: a drowned fisherman from a weave none of you will ever see, sewn down gentle into thin cloth at the harbor's heart, and MET. Fed. Named. Taught the word for “morning” before anyone asks him for anything. He weeps the way you never let yourself, and a province's worth of mended sky stands over him while he does it.`)+P(`The fraying ends not because the door shut, but because the thread finally gets DELIVERED. Heaven's needlework, with a receiving dock. You stand at the crossing-frame most evenings, a Sage with a mended sky and your own line still humming off the hem: home is still there; the light still burns; the door is open both ways now, and some night, on your own schedule, owing nothing, you may yet walk it. Tonight there's a fisherman learning his hundredth word, and the watch to stand.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Morning.`,go:"a5c32_coda"}]};

SC["e_woven_shut"]={img:"heaven",
 t:()=>P(`You close it like a surgeon, not a jailer: when the last drifting soul in sight has settled safe into the harbor of the mended sky, you draw your own gold line back through the door — all of it, hand over hand, forty-some years of slack across the dark — and you sew the selvage shut behind you with the Sage's Shuttle, from inside, the one knot in two heavens nothing can unpick.`)+P(`The porch light is the last thing you see through the closing weave. You watch it until the gap is a seam and the seam is just selvage, and then you stand a long time on the hem of the only world you have now, and you let it hurt exactly as much as it does, which is: enormously, and survivably, in that order.`)+P(`No more arrivals. No more boys on riverbanks, no more Reed Wives screaming in marshes, no more consignments: the Court's entire crime rendered impossible alongside its entire excuse. The mended sky holds on willing thread; the Between, finding no thin cloth here anymore, sends its sutures to other heavens that need them; and the fraying that called your kind across the dark for four hundred years is OVER, because you ended it, from inside, with the door's last keeper's own hands.`)+P(`They will call you the Last Foreigner. Mei calls you to dinner. Most days, that's the whole of it, and most days, that's enough.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Morning.`,go:"a5c32_coda"}]};

SC["e_free_home"]={img:"home",
 t:()=>P(`A world that holds without anyone being indispensable: you built it, you proved it, and then you did the one thing that proves it forever: you left it in charge of itself.`)+P(`The seam-watch took your departure the way a good rota takes anything: into the schedule. Suyin keeps the Sage-craft alive in three apprentices; the ring renews itself at every season-turn now, new knots in new grammars; and the last entry you made in the black reel's silent ledger reads, in your own hand: <i>Sky: holding. Loom: none. Weaver: owed nothing. Gone home.</i>`)+P(`The walk across the Between is long and quiet and entirely yours. Rain on the far side. The bus shelter. The street, smaller than memory and infinitely larger. The porch light, on. You stop at the gate, one hand on the cold wet iron, while two worlds settle into their final arrangement in your chest: behind you, a heaven that learned to hold itself; ahead, a door that was never once locked, kept by someone who never once stopped.`)+P(`You are the only person in two worlds who knows both skies held. It's enough. You open the gate.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Morning.`,go:"a5c32_coda"}]};

SC["e_free_open"]={img:"jianghu",
 t:()=>P(`No loom, no center, no door-keeper: you hold the crossing open and give it AWAY.`)+P(`The harbor rises where the Grand Loom drowned: the valley under the lake, anchors bare, repurposed: arrival-berths where the warp once stood, the Reed Wife's watch-order keeping lamps lit in a place that once banked furnaces. Every soul the Between sends arrives into welcome; every one that wants work gets handed the seam-rota's newest shifts; and the world's weave, held by promises and mended by deliveries, gets STRONGER every season, because the sutures finally land where they're sent and STAY.`)+P(`You stay too, but loose. Unwoven. The jianghu's oldest rhythm, recovered: fix the unfixable, refuse payment, be gone by morning. The Knotless, they still call you on the lower river, a legend with the details wrong and the shape right. You walk the watershed with your gold in your sleeve, mending tears the seam-watch flags, teaching grammar to new arrivals from skies you'll never see, sleeping in ferry-sheds, owed by everyone, owned by no one.`)+P(`And your own line still runs off the hem, humming, toward a porch light. Open is open: both ways, always, for everyone. Some year. On your own schedule. The road decides; that's what roads are for.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Morning.`,go:"a5c32_coda"}]};

SC["e_free_shut"]={img:"jianghu",
 t:()=>P(`The freest sky and the firmest door: you unmade the loom, and then you unmade the need for anyone to follow you here.`)+P(`Sewing the selvage from inside, with no heart-seat and no machine, takes the ring's every knot and the rota's every shoulder and the last full measure of your gold: the greatest single weaving since the Lady's first, and the only one in history performed entirely by volunteers. When it's done, the Between turns its needlework elsewhere, the thin places thicken season by season, and the world is, for the first time in four hundred years, ALONE: one sky, one weave, holding itself, owing nothing to any harvest and nothing to any door.`)+P(`You give the Sage-craft to the seam-watch, the gold's grammar to Suyin's apprentices, and the legend to the river, which was always going to keep it anyway. What's left is a man with quiet hands and two worlds' worth of weather in him, walking a watershed that no longer needs him, which he has decided — slowly, over many ferry crossings — to experience as the victory it is.`)+P(`The porch light burns on, unreachable, remembered, the price of the only sky-tight promise that covered everyone else's little sister too. You tied the knot. You always knew what it cost. Most evenings, watching the seam hold over ten thousand safe roofs, you'd tie it again.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Morning.`,go:"a5c32_coda"}]};

SC["e_heir_home"]={img:"home",
 t:()=>P(`The Lady never managed it. You are not the Lady: you were woven in LIVING, and a living pattern can do what dead pattern dreams about. You split the line.`)+P(`It takes the heart-seat's whole first year to learn, and Suyin weeping with laughter at the audacity, and the ring holding your tension while you work the finest weave of two lifetimes: your own gold, divided at the root the way the Shuttle divides nothing else — the SAGE woven into the heart of heaven, holding, mending, keeping the watch of centuries; and the BOY, whole, separate, fourteen in every way that ever mattered, standing at the Door Between with rain on the far side.`)+P(`You meet yourself's eyes once — there is no protocol for it; you both grin, which is very you — and then the boy steps through, and the heart of heaven holds the door steady for him the entire way across the dark, the longest gentlest Snare ever tied, all the way to a wet street and a gate and a porch light and a door opening before he knocks.`)+P(`The heart-seat feels him land. Feels the line go warm at the far hem: nightly, for years, two worlds' bedtimes almost rhyming. The stitch that stays holds the sky that the stitch that went grew up under, in another heaven, where a sister keeps a light on for a brother who came home strange and quiet and FINALLY all the way present. The Loom remembers its stitches. Both of them. That was always the whole design.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Morning.`,go:"a5c32_coda"}]};

SC["e_heir_open"]={img:"throne",
 t:()=>P(`The heart of heaven, with the door propped wide: you hold the sky AND the welcome, and the two jobs turn out to be one job.`)+P(`From the heart-seat, the whole weave is your skin: every thin place, every season's strain, and every arrival the Between sews down, felt the instant it lands, met before it wakes. No more Reed Wives screaming unheard in marshes; the heart FEELS them land and sends the watch. The harbor rises around the crossing-frame; the rota turns; the ring renews; and the Court's old white city becomes the strangest thing in two heavens' history: a customs-house for souls, administered by reformed believers, audited — forever, at his own insistence — by Iron Veil Ruan.`)+P(`You hold it all living, the pattern's own grammar, and the load is real and shared and survivable: the Lady held alone and broke into thread; you hold in company and stay a man. Mei makes the heart-gallery smell of feverfew. Bao's supply line never once fails. The watch changes; the chord turns; the souls arrive and are named.`)+P(`And on the far hem, a porch light, felt nightly through your own undivided line: unreachable now, and never once unanswered: you learned, in the seat's second winter, to make the line PULSE, gently, on the old schedule. A knock on a door between worlds. Somebody knocks back. Every night. It's not enough and it's everything, in that order, forever.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Morning.`,go:"a5c32_coda"}]};

SC["e_heir_shut"]={img:"throne",
 t:()=>P(`The heart takes the sky; the sky takes no more strangers; and the boy in the heart-seat is the last foreigner heaven will ever send this world.`)+P(`You sew the selvage from the seat itself — the only position in two heavens with the reach to do it clean — and the closing is the new pattern's masterwork: no door, no thin cloth, no fraying call across the dark. The Between's needlework turns to other torn skies. This one is HELD, from inside, by a living heart that mends instead of spends, woven round with a ring of freely-given knots, watched by a rota that will train its grandchildren.`)+P(`What remains is the work, and the work is good: centuries of it, seasons turning, the chord deepening as new willing lines join, the old Court's ledgers re-filed, by Ruan, immortally smug, under RESTITUTION. Suyin's apprentices become masters; the Reed Wife's watch becomes a contemplative order with excellent congee; the legend of the gold thread passes into opera, wrong in every detail, right in the one that counts.`)+P(`The porch light you carry now the way Sho carried a courtyard: inside, intact, load-bearing. The line is sewn into the selvage with everything else — not cut; nothing grasped is ever wholly lost — and some nights, on watch over ten thousand safe roofs, the heart of heaven feels it hum. A boy is owed a light. A world is owed a sky. You only ever had the one pair of hands, and you have never once been sorry where you put them.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Morning.`,go:"a5c32_coda"}]};